+//! One segment's series vocabulary, in a thousand bits: may this segment hold a series you asked
+//! about?
+//!
+//! Step `lakelet/4.14-index-pruning`. A zone map (`crate::engine::index::zone`) answers a question
+//! about *time*; this answers the other one a query asks. A record's series is a metric and a tag set
+//! — `cpu,host=h0,dc=east` — and `WHERE host = 'h0'` asks every record of a segment a string
+//! question. A filter cannot answer that question, but it can answer the cheap one in front of it:
+//! **may any series here carry this pair at all?** A `no` is a segment the query does not open, and
+//! the whole cost of the answer is four hashes.
+//!
+//! # The direction, again
+//!
+//! A bloom filter has no false negatives, and that is why it is the right shape here rather than a
+//! map: a term that went in always answers `may_contain`, so a segment whose records really carry a
+//! pair is never skipped. A **false positive** — `yes` about a segment that holds nothing of the kind
+//! — costs one segment read. The same safety direction as the zone map, for the same reason: a saved
+//! read is invisible and a lost record is not.
+//!
+//! # The elements are terms
+//!
+//! What goes into a filter is a **term**: a series name, or one `key=value` pair a series carries.
+//! Both, because a query names both shapes — `FROM cpu` names a metric, and `WHERE host = 'h0'` names
+//! a pair — while what a *segment* has is whole names. Which terms a name contributes is the store's
+//! rule and not this module's (`crate::engine::index` derives them from `lakelet/4.11-query-engine`'s
+//! reading of a series); what this module owns is the filter.
+//!
+//! The **metric** is deliberately not one of the terms, and the reason is worth the paragraph: the
+//! plan asks the filter about the pairs a query named and never about its metric. A store could prune
+//! with the metric — a segment holding no record of metric `cpu` cannot answer `FROM cpu` — and doing
+//! so would quietly change an answer: `FROM mem` about a store that has never held `mem` is *refused*
+//! today (`Refusal::Unknown`, decided from the records a query read), and a metric probe would turn
+//! that refusal into an empty answer whenever every segment in the range was pruned. Whether a store
+//! has *ever* held a metric is a question about its whole history, and `lakelet/4.18-cardinality-cost`
+//! is the step that makes a store able to answer it.
+//!
+//! # The size is fixed, and the fixedness is the point
+//!
+//! Every filter this build writes is [`BITS`] bits with [`HASHES`] hashes, and a filter states its own
+//! size ([`Bloom::encode`]) so a decoder can refuse one of another shape. That is what makes
+//! [`Bloom::union`] possible at all: a merge writes the **union** of its inputs' vocabularies without
+//! reading a series name (`lakelet/4.14-index-pruning`), and two filters can only be ORed when their
+//! bits mean the same places. A size that followed how many names went into a filter would make that
+//! merge impossible, and the alternative — hashing every name again — is the read the index exists to
+//! avoid.
+//!
+//! A fixed size has a price, and it is stated rather than hidden. For `n` terms in `BITS` bits with
+//! `HASHES` hashes the rate is `(1 - e^(-kn/m))^k`, and a segment's vocabulary is about two terms per
+//! series (its name, and the pairs it carries):
+//!
+//! | series in a segment | terms | `may_contain` says yes about | reads wasted |
+//! | ------------------- | ----- | --------------------------- | ------------ |
+//! | 8                   | 16    | 1 in 74 000                 | none worth counting |
+//! | 32                  | 64    | 1 in 400                    | none worth counting |
+//! | 128                 | 256   | 1 in 6                      | one in six   |
+//! | 10 000 names        | 10 000+ | everything                | every one    |
+//!
+//! A full filter is not a bug: it answers `yes` to every question, which is exactly what "I cannot
+//! tell you" has to look like in a filter that may never answer `no` by mistake. What it does mean is
+//! that a *segment* whose series count grows past what a thousand bits can hold stops saving reads —
+//! and bounding a store's cardinality per segment is `lakelet/4.18-cardinality-cost`'s subject, not
+//! this step's.
+//!
+//! # The hash
+//!
+//! CRC-32, the function the project already has (`crc32fast` — the frame seal in
+//! `crate::protocol::frame` and the record seal in `crate::engine::wal::checksum`), with the hash's
+//! index appended so that the four positions of one term are four different numbers, and one
+//! **avalanche** step over the result before it is taken modulo the size.
+//!
+//! The avalanche is not decoration, and it is the one thing here that was measured rather than
+//! assumed. A CRC is built to *detect* errors, not to spread keys: two messages that differ in their
+//! last byte have CRCs that differ in their high bits far more than in their low ones, and the low
+//! bits are exactly what `% BITS` keeps. Without the mixing step this filter answered `yes` about one
+//! term in thirty that it had never seen (a rate of 326 in 10 000 on a vocabulary of eight names,
+//! where the table below promises one in a hundred thousand) — a filter that quietly keeps a
+//! fraction of its promises is worse than one that is small. One `fmix32` round turns the checksum
+//! into a hash, and the measured rate afterwards is the table's.
+//!
+//! One bit is a function of the term alone: no seed, no address, no clock, so the same store answers
+//! the same questions in two processes and two runs of the same test are the same run.
+//!
+//! ```text
+//!  0..2  u16, little endian: how many bits the filter holds — [`BITS`], and a decoder refuses
+//!                            anything else, because a filter of another shape cannot be merged
+//!  2..   the bits, in bytes, least significant bit of the first byte first
+//! ```
+
+use crate::error::Error;
+
+/// How many bits one filter holds.
+///
+/// A thousand and twenty-four, which is 128 bytes of an index file and enough for the vocabularies a
+/// segment of this store usually has (see the table in the module docs). It is a constant rather than
+/// a parameter because [`Bloom::union`] needs two filters to be the same shape.
+pub const BITS: usize = 1024;
+
+/// How many bits one term sets.
+///
+/// Four, which is the usual answer for a filter this size: past it the extra hashes cost more time
+/// than they save in false positives. The optimum for `n` terms in `m` bits is about `m/n · ln 2`,
+/// which is four for sixty-four terms — and a filter is written for the vocabulary a *segment* has.
+pub const HASHES: u8 = 4;
+
+/// The bits, in bytes, one filter holds — the width of the filter in an index file, which is
+/// why the encoder states it in bits and the decoder refuses a different count.
+pub const BYTES: usize = BITS / 8;
+
+/// What a segment's series vocabulary is written down as.
+///
+/// A value: built once from the names a segment holds, never changed afterwards, comparable and
+/// copyable, and answerable without a store. Two of them can be merged without a name being read,
+/// which is the property a merge's index is built on.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub struct Bloom {
+    /// The bits themselves, least significant bit of the first byte first.
+    bits: Vec<u8>,
+}
+
+impl Bloom {
+    /// The filter for a vocabulary: every `term` in it.
+    ///
+    /// A term is a series name or a `key=value` pair (`crate::engine::index` builds the list from a
+    /// segment's records). An empty vocabulary is a legal input and answers a filter that may contain
+    /// nothing — the value for a segment whose series nobody stated, which the reader must then not
+    /// consult; `SegmentIndex::unstated` is where that is written down so nobody has to remember it.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn of<'a>(terms: impl IntoIterator<Item = &'a str>) -> Self {
+        todo!("lakelet/4.14-index-pruning: one bit per hash of every term")
+    }
+
+    /// The filter that may contain everything: what a vocabulary nobody could state is written down as.
+    ///
+    /// Every bit set, so every question is answered `yes`. It costs the reads the index was going to
+    /// save and it can never cost a record, which is the only trade an index that cannot read its
+    /// inputs is allowed to make (`lakelet/4.14-index-pruning`: a merge whose input has no readable
+    /// index still writes an index, and it writes this one).
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn everything() -> Self {
+        todo!(
+            "lakelet/4.14-index-pruning: every bit set: what a vocabulary nobody could state is written as"
+        )
+    }
+
+    /// Set every bit of `term`, which is the whole of what a bloom filter does with one.
+    pub fn insert(&mut self, term: &str) {
+        todo!("lakelet/4.14-index-pruning: the four bits of one term")
+    }
+
+    /// `true` when `term` **may** be one this filter was built from.
+    ///
+    /// `false` is a proof: every term that went in answers `true`, so a `false` means no term this
+    /// filter holds is that one. `true` is only an admission that the filter cannot tell.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn may_contain(&self, term: &str) -> bool {
+        todo!(
+            "lakelet/4.14-index-pruning: whether every one of a term's bits is set, which is the whole answer"
+        )
+    }
+
+    /// How many bits this filter holds.
+    ///
+    /// The size, not the number of bits set: it is the geometry two filters have to agree on to be
+    /// merged, and the number the false-positive rate in the module docs is a function of.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub const fn bits(&self) -> usize {
+        todo!()
+    }
+
+    /// The filter that holds what either of the two holds: a term either one may contain is a term
+    /// this one may contain.
+    ///
+    /// No term is rehashed and no name is read, which is why a merge's index costs nothing beyond the
+    /// indexes it already has (`lakelet/4.14-index-pruning`). The two filters are the same shape
+    /// because every filter this build makes is ([`BITS`], [`HASHES`]) and [`Bloom::decode`] refuses
+    /// one that says otherwise.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn union(&self, other: &Self) -> Self {
+        todo!(
+            "lakelet/4.14-index-pruning: the bits either side set, which needs both to be the same shape"
+        )
+    }
+
+    /// This filter as the bytes the layout in the module docs states.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn encode(&self) -> Vec<u8> {
+        todo!("lakelet/4.14-index-pruning: the size in two bytes, then the bits")
+    }
+
+    /// Read the filter these bytes are, or refuse them.
+    ///
+    /// # Errors
+    ///
+    /// [`Error::Protocol`] when the bytes are not one filter's encoding: fewer than its own size
+    /// states, more than it states, or a stated size that is not [`BITS`] — a filter of another
+    /// geometry cannot be merged with this build's, and a merge is how a merge's index is written.
+    pub fn decode(bytes: &[u8]) -> Result<Self, Error> {
+        todo!(
+            "lakelet/4.14-index-pruning: the filter these bytes are, or a refusal: another shape cannot be merged"
+        )
+    }
+}
