+//! A sidecar index beside the segments: what a segment holds, written down so that a query can decide
+//! not to open it.
+//!
+//! Step `lakelet/4.14-index-pruning`. `lakelet/4.11-query-engine` prunes from the manifest — a
+//! segment's span of timestamps rules it out of a query's range — and everything a manifest states
+//! about a segment is a *count*: how many bytes, how many records, when the first and last of them
+//! happened. Nothing in it says what the records *are*, so a query with a selective predicate opens
+//! every segment its range meets and throws most of the records away. This module is the third thing
+//! a store knows about a closed segment, and it is a **value**: the zone map of its two ordered columns
+//! ([`zone::Zone`]) and its series vocabulary as a filter ([`bloom::Bloom`]).
+//!
+//! # It is derived state, and that is the central rule of this step
+//!
+//! Everything here can be built again from the segment it belongs to. That has a consequence that the
+//! whole design is shaped around: **a missing, damaged or stale index costs pruning and nothing else.**
+//!
+//! * **missing** — a store whose index directory was deleted still answers every query exactly what it
+//!   answered before, having opened more segments;
+//! * **damaged** — a file that is not an index this build can read is a *hint that is wrong* and not a
+//!   store that is wrong. It is refused ([`SegmentIndex::decode`], checked against a seal from
+//!   `crate::engine::wal::checksum`) and the segment is opened;
+//! * **stale** — an ordinal the manifest does not name. Ordinals are never reused
+//!   (`lakelet/4.8-compaction`), so a file left behind by a merge or a retention pass names a segment
+//!   that is gone, and [`load`] is only ever asked about the ordinals the manifest names.
+//!
+//! The counters say which of the three happened (`index_missing`, `index_unusable`, and
+//! `blocks_pruned` for what the index *did* save — `lakelet/4.14-index-pruning`'s `Outcome`) rather
+//! than an answer changing, because the two are different facts about a store: one says "this store
+//! lost its index", the other says "this store is guessing at it". Neither is a reason to answer with
+//! a hole.
+//!
+//! # Where it lives, and why it is not with the segments
+//!
+//! ```text
+//!  <store>/segments/<n>.lks          one closed segment per file, named by its ordinal
+//!  <store>/index/<n>.lki             the sidecar index of that segment
+//!  <store>/segments.manifest         what the store is: an epoch, a floor and the segments
+//! ```
+//!
+//! Its own directory, beside `segments/`, because the manifest is the store
+//! (`lakelet/4.8-compaction`): a segment counts exactly when the manifest names it, and everything
+//! else in `segments/` is an orphan a crash left behind and is deleted on the next open. An index file
+//! in there would be deleted by that rule the moment it mattered — and the count of files in
+//! `segments/` is a number `lakelet/4.8-compaction`'s and `lakelet/4.9-retention`'s cases assert.
+//! Nothing deletes an index file today: a merge's inputs and a retention pass's segments leave theirs
+//! behind as *garbage*, named by ordinals nothing names any more, which costs a few kilobytes and can
+//! never be consulted. Turning that garbage into a number somebody manages is
+//! `lakelet/4.20-capacity-model`'s subject.
+//!
+//! The index is written **after** the segment's file and **before** the manifest names the segment, so
+//! a crash between the two leaves an index nobody consults rather than a consultation of an index for
+//! a segment the store does not have. The manifest stays the store: what a store *is* never depends on
+//! an index file existing.
+//!
+//! # The layout, byte for byte
+//!
+//! ```text
+//!  0..4        `MAGIC`: the four bytes that say this file is this store's index
+//!  4           `VERSION`: the layout's version, one byte — this build writes 1
+//!  5..9        u32, little endian: how many bytes the zone map's encoding takes
+//!  9..9+z      the zone map (`zone::Zone::encode`)
+//!  9+z..       the series filter (`bloom::Bloom::encode`, which states its own size)
+//!  then        u32, little endian: the seal over everything above (`wal::checksum`)
+//! ```
+//!
+//! A magic and a version of its own, and not the segment's: a file that is not this build's index is
+//! refused rather than read as one, and the two formats have no reason to move together. What a version
+//! buys is the same thing it buys `lakelet/4.7-columnar-segments` and `lakelet/4.9-retention`: an
+//! older layout is a *refusal* rather than a misreading. The seal covers every byte above it, so a
+//! flipped bit in an index file is a damaged hint and not a plausible lie — which for derived state is
+//! the difference between a counting mistake and a wrong answer.
+//!
+//! # What the index is *not*
+//!
+//! It is not a second store and it does not hold a record. Deleting every file below
+//! `<store>/index/` costs a query reads and nothing else, which is why nothing here has an error
+//! path out of [`load`]: an index that cannot be read is a fact about the index, and the plan's
+//! counters are where it is told.
+
+pub mod bloom;
+pub mod zone;
+
+use std::path::{Path, PathBuf};
+
+use crate::error::Error;
+use crate::protocol::batch::Record;
+
+use bloom::Bloom;
+use zone::Zone;
+
+/// The directory inside the store directory that holds the sidecar indexes.
+pub const DIRECTORY: &str = "index";
+
+/// The extension an index file carries: lakelet index.
+pub const EXTENSION: &str = "lki";
+
+/// The four bytes in front of every index this build writes.
+const MAGIC: [u8; 4] = *b"LKLI";
+
+/// Version of the layout in the module docs. A file that says anything else is not this build's index.
+pub const VERSION: u8 = 1;
+
+/// How many digits an index file's name has, so a directory listing is in the ordinals' order — the
+/// same width `lakelet/4.8-compaction` gives a segment's file.
+const DIGITS: usize = 10;
+
+/// The length field in front of the zone map's bytes.
+const LENGTH: usize = 4;
+
+/// The bytes before an index's zone map: [`MAGIC`], [`VERSION`] and that length.
+const HEADER: usize = MAGIC.len() + 1 + LENGTH;
+
+/// The file one segment's index lives in: `<directory>/index/<ordinal>.lki`.
+///
+/// Public for the reason `lakelet/4.8-compaction`'s `file_of` is: the layout is part of what the store
+/// *is*, and an operator with `ls`, a repair tool and a case that has to damage one of these files all
+/// need to name it. The extension is what keeps a listing of the directory readable — `ls` shows where
+/// the segments are and where their indexes are without either being confused for the other.
+#[must_use]
+pub fn file_of(directory: &Path, ordinal: u64) -> PathBuf {
+    todo!("lakelet/4.14-index-pruning: the file one segment's sidecar lives in")
+}
+
+/// What consulting one segment's index found.
+///
+/// Three answers and not two, because `lakelet/4.14-index-pruning`'s counters distinguish them: a
+/// store that lost its index and a store whose index was damaged are different facts, and a reader
+/// that could not tell them apart could not say which one to go and fix.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq)]
+pub enum Lookup {
+    /// The index this store wrote for that ordinal.
+    Found(SegmentIndex),
+    /// No index file for it, or one that cannot be read at all. Pruning is what is lost, and the count
+    /// is `index_missing`.
+    Missing,
+    /// A file is there and is not an index this build can read: damaged bytes, a layout this build does
+    /// not know, or a file cut short. Pruning is what is lost, and the count is `index_unusable`.
+    Damaged,
+}
+
+impl Lookup {
+    /// The index, when there was one this build could read.
+    ///
+    /// The two ways of having none — `Missing` and `Damaged` — are one answer to a caller that only
+    /// wanted to consult an index, and two different answers to a caller that wants to know what is
+    /// wrong with a store. That is why this is a method on the caller's side of the distinction
+    /// rather than a field of it.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn into_index(self) -> Option<SegmentIndex> {
+        todo!("lakelet/4.14-index-pruning: the index, when there was one to read")
+    }
+}
+
+/// One segment's index: what its records span, and which series they may carry.
+///
+/// The two halves answer the two questions a plan asks before opening a file — *may a record here be
+/// inside my range* ([`SegmentIndex::may_meet`]) and *may a series here carry the pair I asked about*
+/// ([`SegmentIndex::may_hold`]) — and both are written in the safe direction: `false` is a proof, and
+/// `true` is only an admission that this index cannot tell.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq)]
+pub struct SegmentIndex {
+    /// The bounds of the segment's timestamps and values.
+    zone: Zone,
+    /// The series names its records carry, and the tag pairs those names spell.
+    series: Bloom,
+}
+
+impl SegmentIndex {
+    /// The index of `records`.
+    ///
+    /// The vocabulary is the **names and the pairs**: a record's series contributes itself and each
+    /// `key=value` pair it carries, read exactly as `lakelet/4.11-query-engine` reads a series — what a
+    /// tag is, is that step's decision, and a store that decided again would prune on a term a query
+    /// never asks about.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn of(records: &[Record]) -> Self {
+        todo!(
+            "lakelet/4.14-index-pruning: the zone of the records and the vocabulary of their series"
+        )
+    }
+
+    /// The index of a segment nobody could state: a zone that meets every range and a filter that may
+    /// contain every term.
+    ///
+    /// This is what a merge writes for a segment whose input has no readable index
+    /// (`lakelet/4.14-index-pruning`), and it is the *safe* answer rather than the convenient one: the
+    /// merged file holds records whose vocabulary nobody wrote down, and an index that claimed to know
+    /// it — an empty filter, say — would prove that those records are not there. Pruning nothing costs
+    /// a read; pruning wrongly costs a record.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn unstated() -> Self {
+        todo!(
+            "lakelet/4.14-index-pruning: the index that prunes nothing, which is the safe answer for what nobody wrote down"
+        )
+    }
+
+    /// The bounds of the segment's timestamps and values.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub const fn zone(&self) -> &Zone {
+        todo!()
+    }
+
+    /// The filter over the segment's series vocabulary.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub const fn series(&self) -> &Bloom {
+        todo!()
+    }
+
+    /// `true` when a record here **may** have a timestamp inside the half-open `[from, to)`.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn may_meet(&self, from: u64, to: u64) -> bool {
+        todo!("lakelet/4.14-index-pruning: may a record here be inside the range")
+    }
+
+    /// `true` when a series here **may** carry the tag pair `pair`, spelled `key=value`.
+    ///
+    /// The spelling is the query's and not the record's: `WHERE host = 'h0'` names the pair
+    /// `host=h0`, and the term the writer put in the filter for a record whose series is
+    /// `cpu,host=h0,dc=east` is that same string. Anything the grammar can spell as one pair is a term
+    /// somebody inserted.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn may_hold(&self, pair: &str) -> bool {
+        todo!("lakelet/4.14-index-pruning: may a series here carry this tag pair")
+    }
+
+    /// The index that covers both segments: the zones' union and both vocabularies.
+    ///
+    /// This is what makes a merge's index free: the vocabulary of the segment a merge writes is the
+    /// union of its inputs' vocabularies, so a merge that already has the inputs' indexes never reads a
+    /// *series name* to write one — the same trick `lakelet/4.9-retention` used for the time range a
+    /// merge's entry states.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn union(&self, other: &Self) -> Self {
+        todo!("lakelet/4.14-index-pruning: the index that covers both segments")
+    }
+
+    /// This index as the bytes the layout in the module docs states: the header, the zone map, the
+    /// filter, and the seal over all of it.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn encode(&self) -> Vec<u8> {
+        todo!("lakelet/4.14-index-pruning: the header, the zone map, the filter and the seal")
+    }
+
+    /// Read the index these bytes are, or refuse them.
+    ///
+    /// Every byte is checked by one of four comparisons, and the order is cheapest first: the file is
+    /// long enough to be an index at all, it starts with this build's magic and version, its seal
+    /// verifies over everything above it, and the two halves decode within the room the header states.
+    ///
+    /// # Errors
+    ///
+    /// [`Error::Protocol`] when the bytes are not this build's index: a magic that is not this store's,
+    /// a layout version this build does not write, a seal that does not verify, a zone or a filter that
+    /// does not decode, or bytes after the seal — an index with something appended is refused rather
+    /// than read as one that is whole (`lakelet/4.7-columnar-segments` refuses a segment the same way).
+    pub fn decode(bytes: &[u8]) -> Result<Self, Error> {
+        todo!(
+            "lakelet/4.14-index-pruning: the index these bytes are, or a refusal: magic, version, seal, then the two halves"
+        )
+    }
+}
+
+/// The index of segment `ordinal`, or why there is none to consult.
+///
+/// This is the only reader, and it has **no error path on purpose**: an index is derived state
+/// (`lakelet/4.14-index-pruning`), so a file that is absent, unreadable or damaged is a fact a plan
+/// counts and does without — never a failure it raises at somebody who asked a question about their
+/// own data.
+#[must_use]
+pub fn load(directory: &Path, ordinal: u64) -> Lookup {
+    todo!("lakelet/4.14-index-pruning: the index of one segment, or why there is none to consult")
+}
+
+/// Write `index` as the index of segment `ordinal`.
+///
+/// Called by the store's writer after the segment's file is on the device and before the manifest names
+/// it (`lakelet/4.8-compaction`'s order for everything a merge or a flush installs), so a crash between
+/// the two leaves a file nobody consults.
+///
+/// # Errors
+///
+/// [`Error::Store`] when the directory cannot be created, or the file cannot be written or flushed. A
+/// store that cannot write down what it knows is a store that answers from what it read, which is why
+/// this is an error and not a shrug: the alternative is a store that silently prunes less for ever.
